<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arsuf</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Arsuf is the site of an ancient and medieval town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> coast north of modern Herzliya, Israel, known in antiquity as Apollonia. Taken by the crusaders in 1101, it was the seat of a lordship of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem</a>, and in 1191 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-i-of-england">Richard I</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> nearby. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baybars">Baybars</a> captured and destroyed it in 1265, and its ruins form Apollonia National Park.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arsuf.jpg" alt="Arsuf" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: אור פ · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arsur_Fortress1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Arsuf was known in antiquity as Apollonia, in the Byzantine period as Sozusa, and to the crusaders as Arsur. It lay on a sandy area on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> coast that ends towards the sea in a cliff, about 34 kilometres south of Caesarea. There is no archaeological evidence for a settlement before the Persian period, around 500 BCE, and the first record of the Greek name Apollonia comes from the mid-4th century BCE. A long-standing suggestion is that the Greek name came from reading the Canaanite god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/resheph">Resheph</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a>, whose name would survive in the later Arabic Arsūf.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Under the Seleucids Apollonia was a port town, and by the mid-4th century BCE it was probably the main city and harbour of the southern Sharon Plain. Under Roman rule it prospered as the chief commercial and industrial centre between the Poleg and Yarkon rivers, though an earthquake partly destroyed it in 113 CE. The Roman proconsul Gabinius found it ruined in 57 BCE and had it rebuilt. By 449 it was called Sozusa and had a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>, and it surrendered on terms to the Sasanian general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahrbaraz">Shahrbaraz</a> in 614. The town fell to the Muslims in 640 and in 809 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a> violently removed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaritans">Samaritans</a> who lived there. Fear of Byzantine fleets led to its first fortified wall.</p><p>King Baldwin I took the town after a siege by land and sea, the crusaders rebuilt its walls and it became the Lordship of Arsur in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem</a>. The Muslims recaptured it in 1187, and it fell again to the crusaders on 7 September 1191 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-arsuf">Battle of Arsuf</a>, fought nearby between the forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-i-of-england">Richard I of England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a>. Balian of Arsuf built new walls, a large castle and a new harbour in 1241, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-ix-of-france">Louis IX of France</a> repaired its ramparts in 1251. From 1261 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-hospitaller">Knights Hospitaller</a> ruled it. In 1265 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> sultan Baibars captured it after 40 days of siege; its inhabitants were killed or sold as slaves, and the town was razed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The site was never again a city, and by the 14th century the geographer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-al-fida">Abulfeda</a> recorded it as without inhabitants. It lies within the municipality of Herzliya, just north of Tel Aviv, and was excavated intensively from 1994. Apollonia National Park opened to the public in 2002. Before the excavations the visible remains included the medieval city wall and moat, a crusader castle with a double wall and a port with jetties sheltered by a sandstone reef.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Apollonia–Arsuf” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Saladin lost Acre to the combined forces of Richard I of England and Philip II of France in 1191 and was defeated in the field at <b>Arsuf</b>, though he retained Jerusalem under the Treaty of Jaffa.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…Fatimids and extending the kingdom along the coast with the help of Genoese, Pisan and Venetian fleets, taking <b>Arsuf</b> and Caesarea in 1101, Acre in 1104 and Beirut and Sidon in 1110, and pushing south-east of the Dead Sea, where he built the castle of Montréal in 1115.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>He went on to win at <b>Arsuf</b> but never retook Jerusalem.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-8">8 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Marching down the coast, Richard's army withstood harassing attacks before a crusader charge broke Saladin's forces at <b>Arsuf</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-7">7 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Arsuf</b>, the ancient Apollonia, surrendered to Baldwin I in 1101 with the help of a Genoese fleet.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-arsuf">Lordship of Arsuf</a></li><li><blockquote>The family survived the catastrophe and in the 13th century dominated Beirut, Jaffa, <b>Arsuf</b> and the Kingdom of Cyprus.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-ibelin">Lordship of Ibelin</a></li></ul></section>
